<Layout title="A home on the internet — Tomas Ferrer" description="Building tomasferrer.com with Astro: a personal space for work, ideas, and curiosity.">
<article class="story"><a class="text-link back-link" href="/#work">← Back to selected work</a><p class="eyebrow">WEB / PERSONAL PROJECT · 2026</p><h1>A home on<br /><span class="serif">the internet.</span></h1><p class="story-lead">A simple place for my work, my ideas, and the person behind them.</p><div class="story-meta"><div><span class="eyebrow">ROLE</span><p>Personal project</p></div><div><span class="eyebrow">BUILT WITH</span><p>Astro · HTML · CSS · TypeScript</p></div><div><span class="eyebrow">STATUS</span><p>First edition</p></div></div><div class="story-body"><h2>The starting point</h2><p>I own tomasferrer.com and wanted to turn it into a personal website: somewhere to introduce myself, collect my work, and gradually share more of what interests me.</p><h2>A clear structure</h2><p>The main reference was <a href="https://www.claytonschoeny.com/">Clayton Schoeny’s website</a>: a persistent profile, direct navigation, and a career story that is easy to scan. <a href="https://juanfeds.com/">JuanFe’s website</a> provided a second idea: giving projects their own pages, with room to explain the thinking behind the work.</p><p>This first version combines a single-page introduction with a separate project story. On smaller screens, the sidebar becomes a compact menu so the content has room to breathe.</p><h2>Small by design</h2><p>Astro generates static pages. A small amount of JavaScript handles the mobile menu and highlights the current section. The design uses a dark green sidebar, a warm paper background, and typography that gives the words space.</p><h2>What comes next</h2><p>The structure is in place. The next step is to add selected analyses and public contact links, then connect the finished site to tomasferrer.com.</p></div><a class="text-link" href="/#work">Back to selected work <span>↗</span></a></article>
</Layout>
